Carafe: the Switch display driver hands Wine the mouse motion it has sent at the end of each pass.
win32u keeps plain mouse motion back until a button, the wheel or MOUSEEVENTF_MOVE_NOCOALESCE asks for it,
as winex11 and winewayland do after their events. The driver never asked, so stick motion reached the game
only with the next click or touch, and the cursor stood still. ProcessEvents now sends a
MOUSEEVENTF_MOVE_NOCOALESCE input after its mouse input, the way those drivers flush.
LGPL-2.1-or-later, as the file it changes.

--- a/dlls/win32u/winnx_drv.c	2026-10-10 03:44:31.854360600 +0400
+++ b/dlls/win32u/winnx_drv.c	2026-10-10 03:44:40.003613200 +0400
@@ -681,6 +681,13 @@
     NtUserSendHardwareInput( 0, 0, &input, 0 );
 }
 
+static void wine_nx_flush_mouse(void)
+{
+    static const INPUT input = { .type = INPUT_MOUSE, .mi.dwFlags = MOUSEEVENTF_MOVE_NOCOALESCE };
+
+    NtUserSendHardwareInput( 0, 0, &input, 0 );
+}
+
 /* Where the cursor really is. NtUserGetCursorPos is not it: without a recent
  * change it asks the display driver, which is this one, and maps the answer
  * through the thread's DPI. The stick works in the screen's own coordinates,
@@ -812,6 +819,7 @@
     if (placed || first) wine_nx_send_mouse( x, y, (placed ? MOUSEEVENTF_MOVE : 0) | first );
     if (stepped) wine_nx_send_mouse_motion( dx, dy );
     if (second) wine_nx_send_mouse( x, y, second );
+    wine_nx_flush_mouse();
     wine_nx_send_extra_mouse( last_buttons, buttons, pressed, released, wheel, hwheel );
     for (unsigned int i = key_after; i < key_count; i++) wine_nx_send_key( key_events + i );
     if (moved || placed || stepped)
